      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1927, part of Ed Sullivan’s ranch became a loading station for the Missouri Pacific Railroad, sparking the town’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sullivancity.org/history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "William Jennings Bryan bought land in Sullivan City in 1910 and opened a store and filling station that served early residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sullivan_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town was officially opened in 1908, marking the start of its development within Hidalgo County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sullivan_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The city is part of the McAllen–Edinburg–Mission and Reynosa–McAllen metropolitan areas, linking it to a larger regional economy.",
